# Coding Standards Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These standards establish consistent formatting, style, and code organization across the codebase. They apply to all Dart and Flutter code, ensuring consistency and maintainability.

## Best Practices
- Follow the official Dart style guide (effective_dart)
- Use the project's linting rules defined in analysis_options.yaml
- Run formatter (flutter format) before committing code
- Fix all linter warnings except where explicitly suppressed with comments
- Use static analysis to maintain code quality
- Keep all imports organized and sorted
- Remove unused code, imports, and dependencies
- Follow naming conventions consistently
- Document public APIs with dartdoc comments

## File / Project Structure Guidelines
- Name files using snake_case
- One class per file for primary classes
- Organize files by feature and layer rather than by type
- Group related files in appropriate directories
- Maximum file length: 400-500 lines (consider refactoring larger files)
- Organize code within files in a consistent order:
  1. Imports
  2. Constants/Configuration
  3. Class definition
  4. Properties and fields
  5. Constructors
  6. Public methods
  7. Private methods
  8. Overridden methods (e.g., build(), dispose())

## Coding & Styling Conventions
- Indentation: 2 spaces (no tabs)
- Line length: 80 characters preferred, 100 maximum
- Use trailing commas for multi-line parameter lists
- Avoid single-line functions except for very simple expressions
- Use braces for all control structures, even single-line statements
- Prefer final for all variables when possible
- Use const constructors when the widget is immutable
- Place closing parentheses on a new line for long method chains
- Use explicit types for public APIs, return values, and complex expressions
- Use meaningful variable names that express intent
- Avoid Hungarian notation (e.g., prefixing types)

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Prefer lazy initialization for expensive objects
- Use const constructors for immutable widgets to improve rendering performance
- Avoid unnecessary widget rebuilds
- Use linting rules to catch common performance issues
- Consider adding performance-specific comments for critical code sections
- Document performance expectations for complex operations

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Run static analysis before committing (flutter analyze)
- Ensure all tests pass before committing (flutter test)
- Format code before committing (flutter format)
- Review changes for adherence to project coding standards
- Validate UI changes on multiple device sizes where appropriate
- Check for internationalization compliance when applicable
- Verify accessibility standards are met
